  "dom_id": "step-q67648",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14163584/assets/6be28d1022cecbbd72a6dd6156f2bc2b6f653416607ae8fa409c25b3e97fdf35.png)\n\nOrder the sides of the triangle $△ABC$ above from the shortest to the longest.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the missing angle $B.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+m∠B+{95}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={180}^{∘}-{135}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠C$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{AB}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSince $∠A$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nBC<AC<AB\n$$\n\nand therefore the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{AB}.\n$$",
